  replacing a blanket lockdown with a  system where anyone who has covered  symptoms gets tested immediately anyone  they've been in contact with also gets  tested  anyone who has it gets isolated and  run teams immediately and that is really  the only way if you look around the  world that you can effectively and  safely exit the kind of lockdown that  we're in safety the first priority but  there is time pressure to lock down in  itself is harmful to the economy and the  longer the shutdown the worse the damage  it's clearly the case of the economic  consequences of the lockdown and of  continuing the lockdown are staggeringly  bad we know that the economy is likely  to shrink over the next two or three  months more than it is essentially ever  shrunk before and it's not just the  economy that will suffer from a long  lockdown our health will too the real  worry is people not going to their GP  when they've got a lump in their breast  not getting spare tests done and in my  area referrals under the two-week cancer  target are down by three-quarters so  that could mean potentially we're  missing three-quarters of the cancers  that we would ordinarily have got we may  well end up with a lot of additional  health problems a lot of additional  deaths in the long run which we won't  they'll bursae that person was killed by  a lot but we will know statistically but  many more people will suffer from  chronic disease suicide but I do think  that those things matter and really do  need to be in what sounds like a very  brutal calculus but I think we have to  be rational and cold-blooded to some  extent we've seen this tension between  economy and health and you know  economists saying it's not such a big  deal and health people saying we need to  lock downs and this is the wrong debate  we should be having the economy of fix  lives and if you want to get through  this faster the fastest way to do it is  to keep numbers lower and so I feel like  everyone wants the same thing which is  if you keep numbers low you can reopen  the economy you can reopen society in a  way that's safe because you can you test  race isolate techniques which only work  if you have a small number of cases we  get to a situation in which the Health  Service begins to become overwhelmed you  immediately will have to get straight  down to another severe lock time  possibly even more severe than this one  an outcome that must be avoided but how  does the government plan to do it does a  proper scaled up testing and tracing  system have to be up and running before  you're even countenance easing the  lockdown do you intend to establish what  you think is a tolerable level of  community transmission or is your aim to  push daily new cases as low as possible  to control the virus both of those  questions are are for ultimately for  politicians because they are the big  judgments what I would say is that test  track and trace can done effectively can  help to suppress the transmission in a  way that allows you then to have lesser  social distancing rules and and  critically test track and trace works  more effectively when the rate of new  cases is lower we're a long way from  normal with no vaccine in sight for a  year perhaps ever we have to learn to  live with kovat 19 the government has  worked hard to keep us locked down in  order to protect the NHS but soon  they're going to have to try to convince  us all that it's okay to come outside  again to places like this and that could  prove difficult people are scared as one  senior minister said to me the messaging  has been stay at home and live go  outside and die and now the government  have to try to tilt that narrative the  cost of this crisis will be countered in  both currency and lives the question now  for the government how much can we  afford better ik b Sky News Westminster
 

